=======================================================
 TERRAFORM STATE FILE & REMOTE BACKEND - STUDY NOTES
=======================================================

1. WHAT IS THE STATE FILE?
-----------------------------------------------------
Terraform's state file (terraform.tfstate) is a JSON
file that maps your .tf config to real-world resources.
It's how Terraform knows what it already created and
what needs to change.

What it tracks:
  - Resource IDs (e.g. actual AWS instance ID for
    aws_instance.web)
  - Resource attributes (IPs, ARNs, etc.)
  - Dependency graph between resources
  - Metadata (provider versions, resource count)

Why it matters:
  - "terraform plan" diffs your config against STATE
    (not against real infra directly) to figure out
    what changed
  - Without state, Terraform has no idea what it
    already made -- it'd try to recreate everything
  - It's the source of truth for destroy, import,
    refresh, etc.

Local state (default) -- the problem:

  project/
    main.tf
    terraform.tfstate   <-- lives on your laptop

  Issues:
  - Not shared -- teammates can't see it
  - No locking -- two people running apply at once
    can corrupt it
  - Often contains secrets in plaintext (DB passwords,
    keys) -- risky if committed to git
  - Single point of failure -- lose the laptop, lose
    the state


2. WHAT IS A REMOTE BACKEND?
-----------------------------------------------------
A backend determines WHERE the state file is stored
and HOW operations (plan/apply) are executed. A REMOTE
backend stores state outside your local machine --
typically S3, Terraform Cloud, Azure Blob, GCS, etc.

Benefits:
  - Shared state -- whole team/CI reads the same
    source of truth
  - Locking -- prevents concurrent applies from
    corrupting state
  - Encryption at rest (S3 SSE)
  - Versioning/history -- roll back a bad state
  - Remote execution (Terraform Cloud/Enterprise) --
    plans run on a remote worker, not your laptop


3. EXAMPLE: S3 REMOTE BACKEND WITH DYNAMODB LOCKING
-----------------------------------------------------

# backend.tf
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket         = "my-company-tfstate-bucket"
    key            = "envs/prod/network/terraform.tfstate"
    region         = "us-east-1"
    dynamodb_table = "terraform-locks"
    encrypt        = true
  }
}

--- Terraform 1.10+ native locking (no DynamoDB) ---

terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket       = "my-company-tfstate-bucket"
    key          = "envs/prod/network/terraform.tfstate"
    region       = "us-east-1"
    encrypt      = true
    use_lockfile = true
  }
}


4. PROVISIONING THE BACKEND INFRASTRUCTURE ITSELF
-----------------------------------------------------
Chicken-and-egg: you need an S3 bucket to store state,
but the bucket is itself a resource. Common pattern:
provision it once separately, then point your main
project at it.

# bootstrap/main.tf -- run this once, separately

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "tfstate" {
  bucket = "my-company-tfstate-bucket"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "tfstate" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.tfstate.id
  versioning_configuration {
    status = "Enabled"
  }
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_server_side_encryption_configuration" "tfstate" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.tfstate.id
  rule {
    apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
      sse_algorithm = "AES256"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_dynamodb_table" "locks" {
  name         = "terraform-locks"
  billing_mode = "PAY_PER_REQUEST"
  hash_key     = "LockID"

  attribute {
    name = "LockID"
    type = "S"
  }
}


5. COMMON COMMANDS
-----------------------------------------------------

# Initialize and connect to remote backend
terraform init

# Migrate from local to remote backend (or vice versa)
terraform init -migrate-state

# See current state contents
terraform show

# List resources tracked in state
terraform state list

# Inspect a specific resource in state
terraform state show aws_instance.web

# Manually remove a resource from state (doesn't
# destroy the real resource)
terraform state rm aws_instance.web

# Pull remote state down to view locally
terraform state pull


6. KEY GOTCHAS (worth remembering)
-----------------------------------------------------
  - NEVER edit terraform.tfstate by hand -- use
    "terraform state" subcommands
  - NEVER commit state files to git if using local
    state -- add *.tfstate* to .gitignore
  - Stuck lock (e.g. crashed process)? Use:
      terraform force-unlock <LOCK_ID>
  - State can contain secrets in plaintext even if
    marked sensitive in config -- encryption at rest
    (S3 SSE) is essential
  - Workspaces vs separate state files: "terraform
    workspace" gives multiple state files under one
    config, but many teams prefer separate "key"
    paths per environment for clearer isolation

